WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 99: The Great Boston Molasses Flood of 1919: The Deadly Tidal Wave of Treacle

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:22.000
[Rebecca] Today, we visit the historic north end of Boston, Massachusetts, on an unusually mild winter afternoon in January 1919, to witness one of the strangest, deadliest, and most surreal industrial disasters in American history.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.720
[Rebecca] The Great Boston Molasses Flood.

5
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:43.040
[Rebecca] In the heart of an Italian immigrant neighborhood, a massive, poorly constructed 50-foot steel storage tank held over 2 million gallons of dark industrial molasses, destined to be distilled into munitions alcohol for World War I when structural fatigue and winter fermentation caused the tank to violently rupture.

6
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:56.960
[Rebecca] An avalanche of 2.3 million gallons of molasses surged through the streets at 35 miles per hour, demolishing buildings, lifting streetcars, and suffocating 21 people in a terrifying brown tsunami.

7
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full astonishing story.

8
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:20.640
[George] Amidst the narrow cobblestone streets, tenement houses, and bakery storefronts stood the busy commercial waterfront of Boston Harbor.

9
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:34.880
[George] In 1915, the United States Industrial Alcohol Company, known as USIA, constructed a colossal steel storage tank on Commercial Street, just steps from the Boston Elevated Railway and the crowded wharves.

10
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:44.080
[George] The tank was a gargantuan cylinder measuring 50 feet tall and 90 feet in diameter, capable of holding nearly two and a half million gallons.

11
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:54.080
[George] It was built for a single, highly lucrative wartime purpose, storing raw fermented molasses imported from Puerto Rico, Cuba, and the West Indies.

12
00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:03.560
[George] While modern consumers think of molasses primarily as a sweet syrup for baking, industrial molasses was a strategic chemical commodity during World War I.

13
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:17.320
[George] By distilling molasses into industrial ethanol, USIA produced alcohol essential for the manufacture of smokeless gunpowder, cordite, dynamite, and high explosives for Allied armies on the Western Front.

14
00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:33.400
[George] Furthermore, with the looming passage of the 18th Amendment establishing national prohibition, USIA was racing against the clock in early 1919 to import as much molasses as possible and distill it into potable alcohol before the nation went legally dry.

15
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:44.280
[George] Financial greed and wartime urgency combined to push production to the absolute limit, with corporate executives turning a blind eye to the lethal time bomb ticking on the Boston waterfront.

16
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:53.800
[George] Wartime munitions contracts generated enormous profits for alcohol distillers, encouraging corporations to disregard basic safety protocols.

17
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:02.040
[George] From the very day it was constructed in 1915, the USIA molasses tank was an engineering catastrophe waiting to happen.

18
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.560
[George] To rush construction, corporate treasurer Arthur P.

19
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:14.280
[George] Gell had supervised the project without consulting qualified structural engineers, naval architects, or city building inspectors.

20
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:30.600
[George] The steel plates used for the tank walls were dangerously thin, measuring only a fraction of the thickness required to hold the immense hydrostatic pressure of two million gallons of molasses, which is 40% denser than water, weighing nearly 12 pounds per gallon.

21
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:37.320
[George] Even worse, the rivets holding the curved steel plates together were undersized and spaced too far apart.

22
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:51.360
[George] When the tank was first filled with a modest test batch of water, leaks appeared immediately along the seams, but USIA officials refused to perform a full hydrostatic pressure test, dismissing the defects as minor blemishes.

23
00:03:51.360 --> 00:04:00.480
[George] As successive shipments of thick molasses were pumped into the tank over the next three years, the structure groaned, shuddered, and leaked continuously.

24
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:10.640
[George] Neighborhood children routinely collected jars of sticky brown molasses dripping from the leaking seams, and local workers dipped their sticks into puddles along the sidewalk.

25
00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.080
[George] Rather than draining the tank, replacing the rivets, and reinforcing the steel walls.

26
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.160
[George] USIA management adopted an insulting cosmetic cover-up.

27
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:29.680
[George] They ordered the entire steel tank painted a dark, mottled reddish-brown so that the chronic leaking molasses would not be visible to the public or municipal authorities.

28
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:38.560
[George] Corporate executives prioritized industrial output over human life, ignoring repeated warning signs and worker complaints for three years.

29
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.720
[George] In early January 1919, the lethal trap was finally sprung by the New England weather.

30
00:04:44.720 --> 00:05:03.680
[George] On January 12, a steamship arrived from Puerto Rico and pumped over two million gallons of freshly brewed, warm tropical molasses directly into the commercial street tank, bringing the total volume to an astonishing 2,300,000 gallons, filling the tank nearly to its 50-foot brim.

31
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:10.800
[George] For the next two days, the warm Caribbean molasses mingled with the cold, viscous molasses already sitting in the tank.

32
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:18.560
[George] Then, on Tuesday and Wednesday, January 14 and 15, a dramatic winter weather shift struck Boston.

33
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:25.280
[George] Temperatures leaped from a bitter 2 degrees below zero up to an unseasonably warm 43 degrees Fahrenheit.

34
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:41.320
[George] This rapid 45-degree temperature change triggered intense internal fermentation within the molasses, generating vast quantities of trapped carbon dioxide gas that exerted immense pneumatic pressure against the already stressed, paper-thin steel roof and walls.

35
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:50.840
[George] At 12:40 on the afternoon of Wednesday, January 15, workers along Commercial Street were sitting outside eating lunch in the mild winter air.

36
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:56.040
[George] Children were playing in the streets, and freight handlers were loading wagons on the wharves.

37
00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:03.960
[George] Deep inside the tank, the sound of machine gun-like popping began as overstressed steel rivets sheared off in rapid succession.

38
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:12.600
[George] Then, with a deafening roar that witnesses described as a cross between a thunderclap and an artillery bombardment, the giant tank ruptured.

39
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:21.000
[George] The sudden temperature spike catalyzed an explosive biochemical reaction that the fatally underengineered steel walls could not contain.

40
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.240
[George] The colossal failure of the steel tank unleashed a horrific physical phenomenon that nobody could have imagined.

41
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:36.520
[George] A 25-foot-high, 160-foot-wide tidal wave of dark, boiling molasses surging through the streets at 35 miles per hour.

42
00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:46.760
[George] Weighing 12,000 tons, the moving wall of viscous fluid possessed staggering kinetic energy, moving with the destructive power of a solid concrete ram.

43
00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:55.160
[George] The wave slammed into the nearby Boston elevated railway trestle, ripping out heavy steel support girders as if they were matchsticks.

44
00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:00.200
[George] An elevated passenger train was approaching the station when the tracks ahead collapsed.

45
00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:09.800
[George] The heroic motorman threw the emergency brakes just in time, bringing the front car to a halt dangling precariously over the surging sea of molasses below.

46
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:20.000
[George] On the street level, the two-story north End firehouse was ripped completely off its foundation and pushed fifty feet into Boston Harbor, with firefighters trapped in the attic.

47
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.600
[George] Tenement buildings, freight offices, and warehouses were pulverized within seconds.

48
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:37.200
[George] Giant jagged shards of the ruptured steel tank, weighing several tons each, were hurled through the air like shrapnel, slicing through telephone poles and slicing into adjacent homes.

49
00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:46.880
[George] A two-ton chunk of steel was blown across the street and buried into an electric substation, severing power lines and igniting fires in the flooded ruins.

50
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:55.360
[George] The kinetic momentum of twelve thousand tons of boiling syrup pulverized heavy infrastructure with the unstoppable fury of an avalanche.

51
00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:04.240
[George] For the pedestrians, horses and workers caught in the path of the wave, survival was a desperate, horrific struggle against an impossible enemy.

52
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:12.000
[George] Unlike water, which flows away and allows victims to swim, molasses is an ultra-viscous, non-Newtonian fluid.

53
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:21.680
[George] As victims struggled to move, the fluid's viscosity increased, locking their arms and legs in an inescapable, suffocating grip like wet concrete.

54
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.840
[George] Dozens of people were knocked down and instantly submerged beneath two to three feet of thick, choking syrup.

55
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] Those who managed to raise their heads found their mouths, nostrils and eyes clogged by the sticky mass, suffocating them where they lay.

56
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:45.600
[George] Ten-year-old Maria Destacio and her friend Pascual Iontosca were walking home from school when the wave engulfed them, both died within minutes.

57
00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.440
[George] Teamsters and their heavy draft horses were trapped in the streets.

58
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:57.040
[George] Over twenty horses were so deeply mired and thrashing in panic that police officers were forced to shoot them to end their suffering.

59
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:05.080
[George] The first responders on the scene were 116 naval cadets from the training ship USS Nantucket, docked nearby.

60
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:07.720
[George] Commanded by Lieutenant Commander H.J.

61
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:17.080
[George] Copeland, the young sailors sprinted into the waste deep morass of molasses, forming human chains to haul semi-conscious victims from the wreckage.

62
00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:26.440
[George] Boston police officers, firemen, and Red Cross volunteers arrived shortly after, but walking through the cooling syrup was nearly impossible.

63
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:32.200
[George] Rescuers were exhausted within minutes, their boots pulled off by the suction of the cooling treacle.

64
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:40.840
[George] Naval cadets and city responders performed superhuman rescue feats, pulling drowning children from the cooling, suffocating morass.

65
00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:49.320
[George] When the rescue operations finally concluded days later, the human toll stood at twenty-one dead and one hundred and fifty injured.

66
00:09:49.320 --> 00:10:00.360
[George] Many of the bodies were recovered days later, so thoroughly coated in hardened molasses that they were unrecognizable, requiring dental records and personal rings for identification.

67
00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:09.320
[George] The final victim, a wagon driver named Cesar Niccolo, was pulled from the waters beneath the harbor wharves nearly four months after the disaster.

68
00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:13.880
[George] The cleanup of the North End was an epic, miserable labor that took months.

69
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:22.040
[George] The cold winter weather caused the molasses to congeal into a thick, rubbery crust that resisted brooms, shovels, and pickaxes.

70
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.760
[George] City engineers finally realized that ordinary fresh water was useless.

71
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:39.000
[George] They brought in city fireboats to pump millions of gallons of pressurized saltwater from the harbor, which dissolved the sticky sugar and washed the sludge into the sea, turning Boston Harbor brown for weeks.

72
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:47.280
[George] Meanwhile, the disaster spread throughout the metropolitan area on the shoes of thousands of spectators, rescue workers and commuters.

73
00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:54.560
[George] Molasses was tracked onto streetcars, subway seats, telephone booths, and living room carpets across Boston.

74
00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:07.600
[George] For decades afterward, residents claimed that on hot, humid summer days, the unmistakable, sweet, cloying aroma of fermented molasses would still rise from the cobblestones and basements of the North End.

75
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:15.440
[George] Saltwater from harbor fireboats proved the only effective solvent against the congealing sugar, which coated neighborhoods for months.

76
00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:26.960
[George] In the immediate aftermath of the catastrophe, the United States Industrial Alcohol Company attempted to evade all financial and moral responsibility by concocting an elaborate defense.

77
00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.800
[George] They claimed that the tank had been deliberately blown up by Italian anarchists and foreign saboteurs.

78
00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:43.040
[George] During the height of the Red Scare, USIA pointed to recent anarchist bombings in New York and Boston, hiring private detectives to find evidence of dynamite residue.

79
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:54.560
[George] However, in 1920, the victims and their families consolidated their lawsuits in the first massive class action tort proceeding in Massachusetts history, appointing former state auditor Hugh W.

80
00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.080
[George] Ogden as the special court auditor to hear evidence.

81
00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:11.920
[George] Over the course of three grueling years, Ogden presided over one of the longest and most thorough trials in American legal history, hearing testimony from over 900 witnesses and filling 25,000 pages of transcripts.

82
00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:19.840
[George] In 1925, Ogden delivered a blistering, historic ruling that completely dismissed the anarchist sabotage theory.

83
00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:31.960
[George] He found USIA solely guilty of gross corporate negligence, proving that the tank was structurally defective, constructed with substandard steel, and overloaded beyond its physical limits.

84
00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:41.560
[George] USIA was ordered to pay $300,000 in damages, equivalent to nearly $5 million today, to the victims and their families.

85
00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:48.280
[George] The Great Boston Molasses flood fundamentally transformed American industrial safety and municipal governance.

86
00:12:48.280 --> 00:13:07.000
[George] In its wake, the city of Boston and municipalities across the nation enacted revolutionary building code reforms, mandating that all commercial structures and storage tanks undergo rigorous engineering inspections, hydrostatic testing, and formal sign-offs by certified professional engineers.

87
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:19.640
[George] Today, a modest green plaque on the Boston Harbor Walk marks the site of the vanished tank, an enduring reminder of 21 innocent lives lost to corporate greed and the day Boston drowned in molasses.

88
00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:30.360
[George] The landmark legal victory held industrial corporations strictly liable for public safety, establishing modern architectural and municipal engineering standards.

